=== printed ===

 show me every kind of formatting you can draw


Laying out one of each.

Everything the renderer knows

A paragraph with bold, italic, struck through, code, a link (https://example.com/one),
a bare autolink https://example.com/two, an image a diagram (diagram.png), and some <b>raw
HTML</b> in the middle of it.

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

An ordered list

1. the first step
2. the second step
3. the third step

A task list

• [x] a thing already done
• [ ] a thing still to do

An indented code block

plain = "indented four spaces, so it has no language"
print(plain)

An HTML block

<div class="note">
  <p>held as it stands</p>
</div>

A table that leans every way

┌──────┬────────┬───────┬──────┐
│ left │ centre │ right │ none │
├──────┼────────┼───────┼──────┤
│ a    │   b    │     c │ d    │
├──────┼────────┼───────┼──────┤
│ aa   │   bb   │    cc │ dd   │
└──────┴────────┴───────┴──────┘

│ a quotation, which the renderer sets in from the edge
│ and which runs to a second line

The end.

A script with a bit of everything in it:

if [[ -d "$DIR" ]]; then
    echo "there"
elif [[ -f "$DIR" ]]; then
    echo "a file"
else
    echo "nowhere"
fi

while read -r NAME; do
    echo "$NAME" > out.txt 2>&1
done < list.txt

until [[ -z "$REST" ]]; do
    REST=""
done

case "$MODE" in
    read)  echo reading ;;
    write) echo writing ;;
    *)     echo other ;;
esac

select CHOICE in one two; do break; done

ls -la | grep -v '^d' | wc -l

And a pattern with a unicode class in it:

^\\p{Lu}\\w{2,8}(?:-\\d+)?$
